WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:00.680
[music playing]

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:01.740
NARRATOR: We humans
are hardwired

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:03.960
to choose mates with the
highest possible value.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.200
[dings]

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.400
Our bio-mall shoppers
are all wearing

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.080
a number between one and six.

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:13.880
Even though they can't
see their own numbers,

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:16.282
Rebel and the others will
pair off and try to make

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:18.752
the highest total number.

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:21.521
Science says that similar
numbers will automatically

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:22.922
pair off with one another--

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:26.626
sixes with sixes, threes
with threes, and so on.

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.728
[applause]

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:29.529
OK.

00:00:29.596 --> 00:00:30.897
So I'm going to put this--

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:32.599
This is different, because
I normally do online shopping.

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:33.400
[laughter]

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:34.334
So.

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:35.435
Going to put
that on your head.

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:36.302
- OK.
- All right.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:37.404
That's OK?
OK.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.972
So you put a
number on my head.

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:40.340
I've put a number
on your head.

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:43.309
Now I'd like to emphasize that
these numbers were randomly

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:46.913
assigned, so don't get bummed
out or impressed with yourself

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.415
when you find out that
you're a one or a six.

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:49.215
REBEL WILSON: Yeah.

00:00:49.282 --> 00:00:50.116
HOST: All right?

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:50.950
When I say go--

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:52.419
So [inaudible], no one's a 10?

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:53.453
[laughter]

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:54.687
You're a 10, Rebs.

00:00:54.754 --> 00:00:55.889
Yeah, yeah, in my heart.

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:56.689
Yeah, thanks.

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:57.490
But in this game--

00:00:57.557 --> 00:00:58.825
[applause]

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:00.293
So you've got 30 seconds.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.694
[drum tapping]

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:02.495
Go.

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:03.430
OK.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.231
So I'm going to
look around, see--

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:06.032
HOST: OK.

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:07.200
REBEL WILSON: --who's got--

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.334
OK.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.503
HOST: So try to make the
highest total possible.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.205
MAN: [inaudible] go past.

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:13.173
REBEL WILSON: Going to go--

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:14.007
HOST: [laughs]

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:15.475
REBEL WILSON: I've got my one.

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:17.010
OK, I'm going to choose you.

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:19.078
HOST: And we're at-- we got 15.

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.514
You got 15 seconds.

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:22.382
Line up behind here.

00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:23.550
Line up right here.

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:24.884
Having headband issues.

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:26.085
And that's good.
Stop right there.

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:27.053
All right.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.321
Got a two and a
three together here.

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:30.924
So you guys came up with a
five out of a possible 12.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:31.758
That's not bad.

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:32.759
Here we got a four and a five.

00:01:32.826 --> 00:01:33.927
That's a nine.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:35.094
Now you took him.

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:36.529
You couldn't find a five?
- Yeah.

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:37.897
It was getting down
to the last minute,

00:01:37.964 --> 00:01:40.967
and you just had to grab the
highest number that was left.

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:41.901
[laughter]

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:42.702
No, it's OK.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:44.304
It's just an exercise.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.805
That's the way it works
in the market, folks.

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:47.073
[laughter]

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.174
So a two and a three.

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:49.409
Were you guys going
for something bigger?

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:50.677
What was happening?
- I was going for bigger.

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:52.912
And then people were,
like, looking at me going,

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:54.013
no, thank you.
- OK.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.315
Oh, that's-- I'm so sorry.

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:58.585
That's not-- well, you guys,
five is better, if I may,

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:01.020
just mathematically
speaking, than a two.

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:02.322
- I was looking for the sixes--
- The last [inaudible]

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:03.756
--and nobody
was looking at me.

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:04.924
HOST: Nobody was looking at you?
- No.

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:06.159
They weren't even giving
you the time of day?

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:06.960
At all.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:07.827
Oh, man.

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:08.995
[whistles] I'm glad I'm married.

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:09.996
[laughter]

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:11.831
This is-- this dating
stuff is tough.

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:13.633
[applause]

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.134
So now Rebel--

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:16.069
Yeah.

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:17.670
--this gentleman
here is a six.

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:19.205
You found a six.

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:20.406
I was decisive too.

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.141
I saw that six, went for it.

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:23.109
Yeah.
Just--

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:23.910
Yeah.

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:25.111
Locked him down.

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:26.045
- Locked him down.
- Yeah.

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:26.880
She got me.

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:27.914
She grabbed you pretty quick.

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:29.015
Mhm.

00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:30.483
She was just like this,
and she went enough,

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:31.918
and then bam, right,
just pulled in there.

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:32.852
Yeah, yeah.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:34.020
Do you know what
number you are?

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:35.154
One?

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:37.223
Rebel, a one?

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.258
You're a six.

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:39.058
Oh.

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:40.460
You are definitely a six.

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:41.261
All right.

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:43.830
So we found-- yeah.

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:45.198
I got a feeling
you would have taken

00:02:45.265 --> 00:02:47.333
her even if she wasn't a six,
if she was lower than a six.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.768
With Rebel, I'm a
winner either way.

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:49.569
Yeah.
Oh, yeah.

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:50.470
That's very sweet.

00:02:50.537 --> 00:02:51.271
Aw.

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:52.071
That is lovely.

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:53.439
[applause]

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.708
They ended up with who they
were supposed to end up,

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:58.278
more or less, from an
evolutionary standpoint.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:02:59.712
The sixes are with
the other sixes.

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:00.980
The ones are with
the other ones.

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:02.282
[music playing]

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:04.250
NARRATOR: This experiment
demonstrates the phenomenon

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.319
known as homogamy.

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:08.655
Some scientists think it
happens because we have

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:12.559
an instinctual preference for
an equally desirable partner,

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:14.627
while others believe we
calculate our own mate

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:16.930
value through
interactions with others

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:19.832
and then settle for the best
mate we think we can get.

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:20.900
[music playing]

